WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:07.280
I have an exciting investment opportunity
only for Two Cents viewers: Beard Oil for dogs!

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.560
I call it Groomio, and it’s a guaranteed success!

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:11.160
How do I know?

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:12.160
Trust me!

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:14.180
I just feel it in my gut!

00:00:19.560 --> 00:00:23.080
No serious investor will trust their money
to your gut.

00:00:23.080 --> 00:00:24.480
And you shouldn’t either.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:28.090
No matter how good you think your idea is,
before you mortgage your house or quit your

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:33.870
day-job, you owe it to yourself to thoroughly
investigate its market viability—that is,

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:39.120
a realistic, data-driven prediction on whether
it can succeed in this marketplace.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.970
This applies to Fortune 500 companies and
handmade soap sellers on Easy.

00:00:43.970 --> 00:00:48.280
One way or another, the market will judge
your product…wouldn’t you rather hear

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:52.260
the verdict before you sink your life savings
into it?

00:01:00.989 --> 00:01:05.380
Ruth here has been giving her homemade artisanal
soaps to her friends and co-workers as gifts

00:01:05.390 --> 00:01:10.860
for a while now, and getting a lot of positive
feedback… enough that she’s actually entertaining

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:12.540
the idea of starting a small business.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:17.549
But before she retires from her office job,
how can she know whether there’s really

00:01:17.549 --> 00:01:18.710
a market for this?

00:01:18.710 --> 00:01:21.530
The answer is market research.

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:25.840
Marketing research companies generate exhaustive
reports on pretty much any industry you can

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:29.340
think of, including the Handmade Global Soap
Market.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:34.080
They include findings like market size, growth
rate, demographics, industry leaders, even

00:01:34.080 --> 00:01:37.130
how facial scrubs perform vs. liquid body
washes.

00:01:37.130 --> 00:01:42.530
And it’s all available to Ruth as a downloadable
pdf for… $4,000.

00:01:42.530 --> 00:01:44.790
Maybe Ruth can afford that, but I can’t.

00:01:44.790 --> 00:01:47.530
Fortunately, there are plenty of cheaper options.

00:01:47.530 --> 00:01:52.960
I found this free report from the non-profit
American Pet Products Association, which says

00:01:52.960 --> 00:01:59.100
that U.S. spending on pet products and services
grew at an average rate of about 5% annually,

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:04.330
comfortably above the GDP average, and that
pet ownership is increasing, especially among

00:02:04.330 --> 00:02:07.200
millennials, with dogs leading the way.

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:11.890
This is all good news for Groomio, because the
bigger a market, the more potential customers,

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:16.349
and the faster it’s growing, the more new
customers—who are much easier to sell to

00:02:16.349 --> 00:02:19.060
than older customers already stuck in their
ways.

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:24.220
The report also tells me that the average
dog owner spends $84 a year on grooming supplies

00:02:24.220 --> 00:02:28.540
and services—very useful info as I decide
on a price.

00:02:28.540 --> 00:02:32.439
A major part of market research is analyzing
competitors.

00:02:32.439 --> 00:02:36.599
If Ruth wants to get into the homemade soap
market, she needs to know what the successful

00:02:36.599 --> 00:02:40.489
brands are, how much they cost, what are the
most popular ingredients and scents.

00:02:40.489 --> 00:02:45.180
She can even request credit reports on these
companies for a fee (unlike personal credit

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:49.690
scores, business credit scores are publicly
available) which will tell her whether they’re

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:53.120
financially stable, and how much risk there
is in the industry.

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:58.120
Search engines and social media have made
collecting this type of information easier than ever.

00:02:58.120 --> 00:03:03.180
A simple Google or Twitter search will often
tell you who the major players in an industry are.

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:08.700
With a GoogleAd account you can see how much
competition there is for your keywords.

00:03:08.709 --> 00:03:13.510
And Google Trends is an easy, kinda fun way
to find out what’s hot and what’s not.

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:16.349
(Seriously, you can burn of lot hours here.)

00:03:16.349 --> 00:03:21.499
I can see that the search terms “dog grooming”
and “beard oil” have both grown slightly

00:03:21.499 --> 00:03:23.049
over the last 5 years.

00:03:23.049 --> 00:03:27.769
I can tell what times of year they’re most
on people’s minds, what geographic regions

00:03:27.769 --> 00:03:31.819
they live in, even what related searches tend
to overlap.

00:03:31.819 --> 00:03:36.440
This can all be useful when designing my product
and advertising strategy.

00:03:36.440 --> 00:03:40.950
All of this is considered secondary market
research, information that already exists

00:03:40.950 --> 00:03:43.069
that you can purchase or find for free.

00:03:43.069 --> 00:03:47.719
But if you’re really serious about success,
you need to do primary market research, which

00:03:47.719 --> 00:03:49.870
means collecting data on your own.

00:03:49.870 --> 00:03:54.449
It’s often more expensive and time-consuming
than secondary market research, but it always

00:03:54.449 --> 00:03:59.209
saves you money in the long run, because you
narrow down your audience and focus only on

00:03:59.209 --> 00:04:01.519
the features that they’re interested in.

00:04:01.519 --> 00:04:06.790
For example, Martin wants to introduce a new
sandwich at his popular food truck: a double-bacon

00:04:06.790 --> 00:04:10.559
BLT with garlic aioli and truffle oil.

00:04:10.559 --> 00:04:15.010
Before rolling it out, he asks his existing
customers to fill out a survey, posts some

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:19.980
polls on his company’s social media accounts,
and just to be really thorough, pays a web

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:24.520
service to get survey responses from the types
of people who frequent food trucks.

00:04:24.520 --> 00:04:30.420
Lo and behold, he finds that people who tend
to like bacon DON’T like truffle oil (because

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:32.140
it’s disgusting).

00:04:32.140 --> 00:04:37.960
He almost wasted a lot of money on an expensive
ingredient that would’ve actually lowered sales.

00:04:37.960 --> 00:04:40.160
Thanks, Primary Market Research!

00:04:40.170 --> 00:04:44.030
Many experienced market researchers will tell
you that when it comes to collecting data,

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:46.220
nothing beats face-to-face conversations.

00:04:46.220 --> 00:04:51.530
That’s because customers are humans with
emotions, and the most successful products

00:04:51.530 --> 00:04:53.530
are ones that solve a pain.

00:04:53.530 --> 00:04:58.310
“Market pains” are anything that causes
frustration or inconvenience in a consumer.

00:04:58.310 --> 00:05:03.410
It’s a problem that your product could potentially
solve, and the best way to find it is talking

00:05:03.410 --> 00:05:05.010
directly to people.

00:05:05.010 --> 00:05:09.580
Big companies conduct interviews with lots
of people of varying demographics and regions,

00:05:09.580 --> 00:05:14.200
but just because you can’t afford that doesn’t
mean you shouldn’t talk to people when you can.

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:17.080
But—don’t bother asking your friends and
family!

00:05:17.090 --> 00:05:22.340
They’re likely to be nice and encouraging
when what you need is brutal honesty.

00:05:22.340 --> 00:05:26.800
For instance, Ruth starts taking her handmade
soaps to the local farmer’s market, and

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:30.710
makes sure she strikes up a conversation with
anyone who’ll give her the time.

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:35.560
She notices a trend in people who say they
wouldn’t buy handmade soap online because

00:05:35.560 --> 00:05:38.310
they’re concerned about the packaging waste.

00:05:38.310 --> 00:05:40.240
Aha, market pain!

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:44.960
She decides to make sure all her packaging
is made from recyclable materials—and that

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:47.350
it’s mentioned prominently in the advertising.

00:05:47.350 --> 00:05:52.820
For the truly introverted entrepreneur, the
internet does offer a sneaky shortcut to finding

00:05:52.820 --> 00:05:56.410
market pain: the dreaded comments section.

00:05:56.410 --> 00:06:01.270
If your competitor has one, you’ll find
people happy to complain about drawbacks or

00:06:01.270 --> 00:06:03.530
features they wish the product had.

00:06:03.530 --> 00:06:08.790
There’s even a successful electronics company
that has made its whole business model developing

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:11.580
products based on complaints in Amazon reviews.

00:06:11.580 --> 00:06:17.160
“I love this nose hair trimmer but I wish
it was waterproof!” Bam, market pain.

00:06:17.160 --> 00:06:20.330
This is a very generalized overview of market
research.

00:06:20.330 --> 00:06:25.120
There’s a lot more to it: industry lifecycle,
market penetration, federal regulations—

00:06:25.120 --> 00:06:26.420
Oh shoot!

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:30.060
I forgot to check if animal cosmetics require
FDA approval!

00:06:30.070 --> 00:06:32.920
Thankfully, there are a lot of resources out
there.

00:06:32.920 --> 00:06:37.810
The Small Business Administration provides
lists of organizations that offer free market

00:06:37.810 --> 00:06:41.270
reports, and can help connect you with a counselor
or advisor.

00:06:41.270 --> 00:06:45.170
But be careful of business coaches who think
their job is to just be your cheerleader.

00:06:45.170 --> 00:06:49.110
While this encouragement is nice, you don’t want
to be encouraged off a cliff.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:50.110
Ooh!

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:51.820
The results of my survey are in!

00:06:51.820 --> 00:06:59.780
The percentage of dog owners who say they’d
be interested in Groom is… 4%.

00:06:59.780 --> 00:07:02.840
Maybe I shouldn’t take out that loan just
yet.

00:07:02.840 --> 00:07:06.790
And that’s the most important part of market
research: objective honesty.

00:07:06.790 --> 00:07:11.430
No matter how much you love your idea, if
the research tells you otherwise, well…

00:07:11.430 --> 00:07:13.050
that can be a good thing!

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:16.460
It means you just saved yourself a lot of
money and effort.

00:07:16.460 --> 00:07:20.300
And freed up your time to develop an idea
that will work.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:21.660
And that’s our two cents!

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:25.720
Thanks to out patrons for keeping Two Cents financially healthy.

